course: dictionary
lesson: 10714
title: Untwist: turning things back
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To untwist something is to turn it the other way so it is no longer twisted. Twist means to wind parts around each other, so untwist means to undo that winding.
explain: «Feather grass seeds have long feathery tails that twist when dry and untwist when damp.» The un- at the front shows the reverse: the tail was wound up, and now it turns back. Ask what word would fit: the tail unwinds, and the seed screws into the soil.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it in the sentence. If «untwist» means turning back, the sentence still makes sense. Since feather grass seeds can stick in dogs' ears, check pets after walks.
fact: Untwist is a verb, so it shows an action: untwist, untwists, untwisted, untwisting.
fact: The prefix un- often means reverse or undo, as in untie and unlock.
fact: «Untwist» means to turn something so it is no longer twisted.
fact: «Feather grass» seeds have long tails that twist when dry and untwist when damp.
fact: Wires, ropes, lids, bags and threads can all be untwisted.
exercise: Mia has a rope wound into a tight coil. She wants to untwist it. What does she do? | Ties it into a knot | Wets it until it shrinks | * Turns it back so it is no longer twisted | Cuts it in half
exercise: «Feather grass seeds have long tails that twist when dry and untwist when damp.» Find and copy the word that shows the tail turning back when it is damp. | feather | * untwist | seeds | dry
exercise: What does the un- in untwist tell you? | The word is a noun | Something is very twisted | * It reverses the action of twist | The word means the same as twist
test: Which thing would you untwist? | A glass of cold water | * A wire wound into a tight spiral | A slice of bread | A sunny day
test: Why should you check dogs after walks where feather grass grows? | Its seeds are good to eat | Its tails make them run faster | Its seeds make them sleepy | * Its sharp seeds can stick in their ears
exam: What does untwist mean? | To count something carefully | * To turn something so it is no longer twisted | To cut something into pieces | To twist something even tighter
